              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Micky Brand"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is the mosy delicious bean salad I've ever tasted! Plus it is quick, easy and inexpensive. Replaced the lima beans with butter beans and used red onion and raw sugar to make it a bit sweeter and it did the trick. Used fresh, steamed green beans instead of canned and tossed it straight from the steamer into the dressing - this helped to heat the sugar a bit to blend into the dressing more easily. Gorgeous! Cover the bowl with some cling film and it will help the absorbtion of the dressing. Just some caution: the dressing is quite acidic. Eat this salad with something starchy like pasta or panini to tone it down a notch.",
            "datePublished": "2014-09-01T06:56:56.630Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "3"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "FizzyMixer5079"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Agree with all who say the sugar is too much; so make the dressing separately with half the amount and mix in sugar to taste. We use *mixed salt* so the sugar is just in the background. All beans are welcomed!",
            "datePublished": "2023-07-20T11:38:43.349Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "1"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "annecan"
            },
            "reviewBody": "That recipe had sooo much sugar, I thought it must be some kind of dessert. No wonder some people said their kids loved it! You could hand it out for Halloween trick or treat candy. Lower it to about a teaspoon or none at all...it is unnecessary.",
            "datePublished": "2018-01-11T18:10:40.260Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Donna Tabone"
            },
            "reviewBody": "The best bean salad ever. The only change i made was cutting the sugar in half. Delicious...",
            "datePublished": "2022-07-04T19:08:59.824Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "sweetpmc"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Good recipe with a few changes. Cut the sugar way down to a tablespoon with 1/2 cup white vinegar and a splash of balsamic. Used a good olive oil instead of vegetable oil. Couldn't find pimentos, so I subbed in chopped red pepper. Added a teaspoon of dried marjoram. Delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2010-12-21T23:06:04.440Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "L. Britten"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Awesome salad! I agree with other reviewers that it's okay to mix and match the veggies according to one's tastes. I used this recipe as a jumping-off point for my own version, with green beans, wax beans, garbanzo beans, dark red kidney beans, and sliced beets. For the dressing, I reduced the sugar to 1/2 cup (I used \"Sugar in the Raw\"), and paired that with 1/2 cup white vinegar and 1/2 cup balsamic vinegar (for an extra kick). I added minced garlic and fresh cracked black pepper before giving everything a toss. This salad gets better with time, so if you can make it a couple hours before you want to serve it, the veggies will marinate in the dressing and it will taste amazing.",
            "datePublished": "2010-07-25T14:08:27.223Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "BramptonMommyof2"
            },
            "reviewBody": "great taste...thought 1 cup of sugar was way too much so I only used half. Still came out great. The leftovers , I added quinoa and it made a filling lunch.",
            "datePublished": "2012-07-10T12:27:55.763Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Judy Ann"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Nice with celery I had never used for crunch and pimento for color…. Very nice touch. I enjoy a little more tart so added sugar 1/4 at a time. Only used a scant 3/4 cup. Very tasty Like many others exchanged wax beans for limas as My grandkids won’t touch the limas. Maybe someday will acquire a taste as I have. I would advise adding sugar slowly to taste. Thank you for sharing your family recipe. Judy",
            "datePublished": "2022-07-03T15:20:55.398Z"
          },
